  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\st0039_32f6d6c139a3.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 140px;\" /></p><p>The potential difference across the branches&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>&nbsp;is zero. So, the equivalent capacitance will be only due to the&nbsp;combination of two capacitors in parallel.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
